\begin_layout Section
The Benchmark Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The benchmark model has three primitives:
 a consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 a utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
 and a feasible-set correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\mapsto B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 At prices and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 the consumer solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{*}\in x(p,w):=\arg\max_{y\in B(p,w)}u(y).
\]

\end_inset

Everything in the chapter follows from this optimization problem plus assumptions that are displayed where they are used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Utility is ordinal in the deterministic benchmark:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing on 
\begin_inset Formula $u(X)$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\circ u$
\end_inset

 generate exactly the same demand.
 We therefore take a utility representation as the primitive here without attaching cardinal or interpersonal meaning to its levels.
 Chapter 4 returns to the logically prior question of when an observed preference relation admits such a representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
