\begin_layout Section
Commodity Space and Budget Sets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An economic commodity is a good or service whose quantity matters to the consumer.
 There are 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq1$
\end_inset

 commodities.
 A consumption bundle is a column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 denotes the quantity of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 records the bundles that are feasible for the consumer.
 Unless a section states otherwise,
 we take 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
 so negative consumption is ruled out.
 Other applications may impose upper bounds or interpret coordinates as net trades.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The consumer cannot generally choose every bundle in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 In the benchmark model,
 prices and wealth are the only constraints.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 denote wealth and let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{++}$
\end_inset

 denote the price vector.
 The budget set is 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

,
 a subset of the consumption set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:consumer-theory-utility-maximization:03:the-utility-maximization-problem
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Utility Maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous and 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The utility maximization problem (UMP) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x\geq0}u(x)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{subject to}\quad p\cdot x\leq w.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalently,
 define the budget set as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 then the UMP has a solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 contains the zero bundle,
 it is nonempty.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and each commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

,
 strict positivity of prices gives 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq x_{\ell}\leq w/p_{\ell}$
\end_inset

;
 hence the budget set is bounded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The map 
\begin_inset Formula $x\mapsto p\cdot x$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}\cap\{x:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

 is closed.
 The Heine--Borel theorem therefore implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is compact.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Weierstrass maximum theorem therefore gives a solution to the UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now prove further properties of the demand correspondence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The Marshallian demand correspondence is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w):=\arg\max_{y\in B(p,w)}u(y).
\]

\end_inset

 It is non-empty,
 compact-valued and upper hemicontinuous.
 The indirect utility function is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(p,w)=\max_{y\in B(p,w)}u(y),
\]

\end_inset

 and it is continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

The result follows from Berge's maximum theorem once we verify that the budget correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 We already know that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous and that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is compact,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is compact-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{n},w^{n})\to(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\in B(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\cdot x^{n}\leq w^{n}$
\end_inset

;
 taking limits gives 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

,
 so the graph is closed.
 Moreover,
 near any 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 prices are bounded away from zero and wealth is bounded above;
 hence the nearby budget sets are uniformly bounded.
 Closed graph plus this local boundedness gives upper hemicontinuity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the reverse inclusion,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x<w$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 for all sufficiently large 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$
\end_inset

,
 set 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{n}=\frac{x}{\max\{1,(p^{n}\cdot x)/w^{n}\}}.
\]

\end_inset

 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\in B(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\to x$
\end_inset

.
 Hence the budget correspondence is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}_{++}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

,
 and Berge's theorem gives the stated properties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next definitions separate the assumptions used in the comparative-statics results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Local nonsatiation.
 A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is locally nonsatiated if,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 and every 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon>0$
\end_inset

,
 there is 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lVert y-x\rVert_{2}<\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If a utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation generates the Marshallian demand correspondence,
 then every demanded bundle exhausts the budget:
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof.
  If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x<w$
\end_inset

,
 local nonsatiation supplies a strictly preferred bundle arbitrarily close to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 For a sufficiently small neighborhood the bundle remains affordable,
 contradicting utility maximization.
  Therefore the budget constraint binds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The same demand correspondence is homogeneous of degree zero.
  If preferences are convex,
 demand is convex-valued;
 if preferences are strictly convex,
 demand is single-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Homogeneity follows because 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\lambda p,\lambda w)=B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and preferences are convex,
 every mixture 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y$
\end_inset

 is feasible and weakly preferred to either maximizer,
 so it is also demanded.
 Under strict convexity,
 two distinct maximizers would have a feasible mixture strictly preferred to both,
 a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
