\begin_layout Section
Workhorse Utility Specifications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A useful specification gives more than a closed-form answer: it identifies the assumptions behind expenditure shares, substitution, and corner choices. We derive several families from the same budget problem. Throughout, prices are strictly positive and wealth is positive; additional consumption-domain restrictions are stated for each model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Homotheticity and Cobb-Douglas Demand
\end_layout



\begin_layout Definition
Homothetic preferences.
 A preference relation is homothetic if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x\succeq\alpha y$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 whenever the scaled bundles are feasible.
 Under the usual continuity and monotonicity assumptions,
 it admits a positively homogeneous utility representation;
 an arbitrary representation need not itself be homogeneous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Cobb--Douglas representation 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2})=x^{\alpha}_{1}x^{1-\alpha}_{2}$
\end_inset

 is a standard example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For \begin_inset Formula $L$\end_inset
 goods, fix positive taste weights \begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{utility-weights}{a_\ell}$\end_inset
 with \begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}a_\ell=1$\end_inset
. Cobb-Douglas utility is \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\prod_{\ell=1}^{L}x_\ell^{a_\ell}$\end_inset
. Every boundary bundle has utility zero and a strictly positive affordable bundle has positive utility, so the optimum is interior. Maximizing its logarithm gives \begin_inset Formula $a_\ell/x_\ell=\lambda p_\ell$\end_inset
. Multiplying by \begin_inset Formula $x_\ell$\end_inset
 and summing gives \begin_inset Formula $\lambda=1/w$\end_inset
, hence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
x_\ell(p,w)=a_\ell\frac{w}{p_\ell},\qquad \notationfirst{expenditure-shares}{b_\ell(p,w)}:=\frac{p_\ell x_\ell(p,w)}{w}=a_\ell.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expenditure share \begin_inset Formula $b_\ell$\end_inset
 is the fraction of the budget allocated to good \begin_inset Formula $\ell$\end_inset
; the share vector is \begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol b=(b_1,\ldots,b_L)^\top$\end_inset
. Cobb-Douglas shares are constant even though quantities respond to prices. Each wealth elasticity is one, each own-price elasticity is minus one, and each uncompensated cross-price elasticity is zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
A fixed budget share. Let \begin_inset Formula $a=(1/4,3/4)$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(2,1)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=80$\end_inset
. Demand is \begin_inset Formula $(10,60)$\end_inset
 and expenditure is \begin_inset Formula $(20,60)$\end_inset
. Doubling only the first price halves \begin_inset Formula $x_1$\end_inset
 while leaving \begin_inset Formula $x_2$\end_inset
 unchanged. Doubling wealth doubles both quantities. A constant share is a restriction on behavior, not a property of all homothetic preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
CES: A Family of Substitution Patterns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constant-elasticity-of-substitution (CES) family varies substitution while retaining explicit demand. Keep the positive normalized weights above. Let \begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{ces-curvature}{\rho}<1$\end_inset
 and define the substitution elasticity \begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{substitution-elasticity}{\sigma}=1/(1-\rho)>0$\end_inset
. For \begin_inset Formula $\rho\ne0$\end_inset
, use
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
u_\rho(x)=\left(\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}a_\ell x_\ell^\rho\right)^{1/\rho}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For \begin_inset Formula $0<\rho<1$\end_inset
 this formula is defined on \begin_inset Formula $\mathbb R_+^L$\end_inset
. For \begin_inset Formula $\rho<0$\end_inset
, define it first on \begin_inset Formula $\mathbb R_{++}^L$\end_inset
 and extend it continuously by assigning zero utility to bundles with any zero coordinate. At \begin_inset Formula $\rho=0$\end_inset
, take the continuous limit \begin_inset Formula $u_0(x)=\prod_\ell x_\ell^{a_\ell}$\end_inset
. Each representation is homogeneous of degree one. CES preferences are therefore homothetic, including cases in which expenditure shares vary with relative prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The optimum is interior. For \begin_inset Formula $\rho\le0$\end_inset
, a positive bundle dominates every boundary bundle in utility. For \begin_inset Formula $0<\rho<1$\end_inset
, a small transfer of expenditure toward a missing good is beneficial because its marginal utility becomes arbitrarily large near zero. For \begin_inset Formula $\rho\ne0$\end_inset
, an equivalent objective on the positive orthant, including the sign needed when \begin_inset Formula $\rho<0$\end_inset
, is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\widetilde u_\rho(x)=\frac{\sum_\ell a_\ell x_\ell^\rho-1}{\rho},\qquad \partial_{x_\ell x_\ell}^{2}\widetilde u_\rho=a_\ell(\rho-1)x_\ell^{\rho-2}<0.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At \begin_inset Formula $\rho=0$\end_inset
, instead use \begin_inset Formula $\widetilde u_0(x)=\sum_\ell a_\ell\log x_\ell$\end_inset
, with own second derivatives \begin_inset Formula $-a_\ell/x_\ell^2<0$\end_inset
. In both cases all cross derivatives vanish, so the objective is strictly concave. Its first-order conditions \begin_inset Formula $a_\ell x_\ell^{\rho-1}=\lambda p_\ell$\end_inset
, together with budget exhaustion, identify the unique maximizer. For negative \begin_inset Formula $\rho$\end_inset
, maximizing the untransformed sum of powers would reverse the intended ranking. Taking ratios and then imposing the budget gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\frac{x_\ell}{x_k}=\left(\frac{a_\ell/p_\ell}{a_k/p_k}\right)^\sigma,\qquad x_\ell(p,w)=\frac{w a_\ell^\sigma p_\ell^{-\sigma}}{\sum_{k=1}^{L}a_k^\sigma p_k^{1-\sigma}}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same demand formula applies at \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1$\end_inset
 and gives Cobb-Douglas. It satisfies Walras' law, has degree zero in \begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$\end_inset
, and scales linearly with wealth. Its shares are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
b_\ell(p)=\frac{a_\ell^\sigma p_\ell^{1-\sigma}}{\sum_k a_k^\sigma p_k^{1-\sigma}}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Except in the Cobb-Douglas case, a taste weight is generally not an expenditure share. Raising a good's price lowers its share if \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$\end_inset
 and raises its share if \begin_inset Formula $0<\sigma<1$\end_inset
, even though its purchased quantity falls in both cases. The name CES refers to the response of a quantity ratio to a relative price:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
-\frac{d\log(x_\ell/x_k)}{d\log(p_\ell/p_k)}=\sigma.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This relative-demand elasticity differs from an individual good's own-price elasticity. Differentiating the demand formula at fixed wealth gives, for \begin_inset Formula $k\ne\ell$\end_inset
,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
\begin{aligned}\frac{\partial\log x_\ell}{\partial\log w}&=1,\\ \frac{\partial\log x_\ell}{\partial\log p_\ell}&=-\sigma(1-b_\ell)-b_\ell,\\ \frac{\partial\log x_\ell}{\partial\log p_k}&=(\sigma-1)b_k.\end{aligned}
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus every good is normal. Distinct goods are gross substitutes when \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$\end_inset
 and gross complements when \begin_inset Formula $0<\sigma<1$\end_inset
: these terms describe the cross-price response at fixed wealth. Chapter 2 separates this response into substitution and purchasing-power effects. In particular, a negative uncompensated cross effect need not imply a negative compensated cross effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Substitution and relative expenditure. Let \begin_inset Formula $a_1=a_2=1/2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\rho=1/2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,2)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=120$\end_inset
. Then \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=2$\end_inset
 and the denominator is \begin_inset Formula $1/4+1/8=3/8$\end_inset
, giving \begin_inset Formula $x=(80,20)$\end_inset
. The quantity ratio is four but the expenditure ratio is two. The budget check is \begin_inset Formula $80+2(20)=120$\end_inset
. The own-price elasticity of good 1 is \begin_inset Formula $-4/3$\end_inset
, rather than \begin_inset Formula $-2$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Gross complementarity. Let \begin_inset Formula $a_1=a_2=1/2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\rho=-1$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,4)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=90$\end_inset
. Here \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1/2$\end_inset
, demand is \begin_inset Formula $(30,15)$\end_inset
, and shares are \begin_inset Formula $(1/3,2/3)$\end_inset
. Raising only \begin_inset Formula $p_2$\end_inset
 to 9 gives \begin_inset Formula $(22.5,7.5)$\end_inset
. Both quantities fall: the greater purchasing-power burden outweighs the relative-price incentive to consume more of good 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Weights versus shares in three goods. Let \begin_inset Formula $a=(0.5,0.3,0.2)$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,1,1)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=38$\end_inset
. The denominator is \begin_inset Formula $0.25+0.09+0.04=0.38$\end_inset
, so \begin_inset Formula $x=(25,9,4)$\end_inset
. Shares are \begin_inset Formula $(25/38,9/38,4/38)$\end_inset
, not the taste weights. The same weights under Cobb-Douglas give \begin_inset Formula $(19,11.4,7.6)$\end_inset
. A change in curvature with weights fixed generally changes the reference allocation as well as price responses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Calibration makes the distinction operational. Given positive reference shares \begin_inset Formula $b_\ell^0=p_\ell^0x_\ell^0/w^0$\end_inset
 and a specified \begin_inset Formula $\sigma$\end_inset
, weights reproducing that observation are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
a_\ell=\frac{(b_\ell^0)^{1/\sigma}(p_\ell^0)^{(\sigma-1)/\sigma}}{\sum_k (b_k^0)^{1/\sigma}(p_k^0)^{(\sigma-1)/\sigma}}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One positive observation therefore cannot identify the substitution elasticity: each specified \begin_inset Formula $\sigma$\end_inset
 admits calibrated weights. Different elasticities can fit the same initial allocation and predict different responses to a price change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Limiting Cases: Substitutes and Complements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Holding weights fixed, \begin_inset Formula $\rho\to0$\end_inset
 gives Cobb-Douglas, \begin_inset Formula $\rho\uparrow1$\end_inset
 gives \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\sum_\ell a_\ell x_\ell$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $\rho\to-\infty$\end_inset
 gives \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\min_\ell x_\ell$\end_inset
. Fixed weights disappear in the last limit. Unequal required proportions instead arise by applying the CES aggregator to \begin_inset Formula $x_\ell/d_\ell$\end_inset
, with \begin_inset Formula $d_\ell>0$\end_inset
, before taking that limit. The endpoint models must be solved directly; their solutions need not satisfy the interior CES conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Perfect substitutes and a demand correspondence. For \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=3x_1+x_2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(2,1)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=12$\end_inset
, utility per dollar is \begin_inset Formula $3/2$\end_inset
 for good 1 and 1 for good 2, so the unique choice is \begin_inset Formula $(6,0)$\end_inset
. At \begin_inset Formula $p=(3,1)$\end_inset
 the two ratios tie: every nonnegative bundle satisfying \begin_inset Formula $3x_1+x_2=12$\end_inset
 maximizes utility. Reporting a single demand formula would omit valid choices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Perfect complements and a kink. For \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\min\{x_1/2,x_2\}$\end_inset
, the consumer buys \begin_inset Formula $x_1=2x_2$\end_inset
. At \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,3)$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=20$\end_inset
, the chosen bundle is \begin_inset Formula $(8,4)$\end_inset
 because \begin_inset Formula $2(4)+3(4)=20$\end_inset
. In general \begin_inset Formula $x_2=w/(2p_1+p_2)$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $x_1=2w/(2p_1+p_2)$\end_inset
. Tangency of differentiable indifference curves is unavailable at the optimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Subsistence and Stone-Geary Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To allow expenditure shares to vary with wealth, introduce a nonnegative subsistence bundle \begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{subsistence-bundle}{\gamma}\in\mathbb R_+^L$\end_inset
. On \begin_inset Formula $X=\gamma+\mathbb R_+^L$\end_inset
, let \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\prod_\ell(x_\ell-\gamma_\ell)^{a_\ell}$\end_inset
 with positive normalized weights. Suppose \begin_inset Formula $w>p\cdot\gamma$\end_inset
. Setting \begin_inset Formula $z=x-\gamma$\end_inset
 leaves discretionary wealth \begin_inset Formula $w-p\cdot\gamma$\end_inset
, so Cobb-Douglas demand in \begin_inset Formula $z$\end_inset
 gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
x_\ell(p,w)=\gamma_\ell+a_\ell\frac{w-p\cdot\gamma}{p_\ell}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At \begin_inset Formula $w=p\cdot\gamma$\end_inset
, the only feasible bundle is \begin_inset Formula $\gamma$\end_inset
; below that level this subsistence-constrained model has no feasible bundle. It is not a model of behavior below subsistence. Except in special cases, these preferences are not homothetic about the origin and their budget shares depend on wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
A necessity and discretionary spending. Let \begin_inset Formula $\gamma=(2,1)$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $a=(1/2,1/2)$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p=(2,1)$\end_inset
, and \begin_inset Formula $w=15$\end_inset
. Subsistence costs 5, leaving 10 to allocate; demand is \begin_inset Formula $(4.5,6)$\end_inset
 and shares are \begin_inset Formula $(0.6,0.4)$\end_inset
. At wealth 25, demand is \begin_inset Formula $(7,11)$\end_inset
 and shares are \begin_inset Formula $(0.56,0.44)$\end_inset
. The first good's share falls even though its quantity increases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Quasilinearity and Wealth-Dependent Corners
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Quasilinear preferences.
 A numeraire good 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is quasilinear when preferences admit a representation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x)=g(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L-1})+x_{L}.
\]

\end_inset

 Such preferences are invariant to a common translation of both bundles in the numeraire whenever the translated bundles remain feasible.
 The converse is not implied by translation invariance alone.
 On a product domain 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\times\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
 one sufficient formulation adds strict monotonicity in the numeraire and numeraire solvability:
 for a fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{0}\in Z$
\end_inset

 and every 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in Z$
\end_inset

,
 there is a unique 
\begin_inset Formula $g(z)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,0)\sim(z_{0},g(z))$
\end_inset

.
 Translation invariance then gives 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,m)\sim(z_{0},g(z)+m)$
\end_inset

,
 which yields the displayed representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A nonnegative numeraire creates a boundary that matters for demand. Let \begin_inset Formula $u(x_1,x_2)=2\sqrt{x_1}+x_2$\end_inset
 on \begin_inset Formula $\mathbb R_+^2$\end_inset
, normalize \begin_inset Formula $p_2=1$\end_inset
, and write \begin_inset Formula $r=p_1>0$\end_inset
. Substituting the budget gives the strictly concave problem \begin_inset Formula $\max_{0\le x_1\le w/r}\{2\sqrt{x_1}-rx_1+w\}$\end_inset
. Its unique solution is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
x_1(r,w)=\min\{r^{-2},w/r\},\qquad x_2(r,w)=\max\{w-r^{-1},0\}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
When zero wealth effects stop applying. At \begin_inset Formula $r=2$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=3$\end_inset
, demand is \begin_inset Formula $(1/4,5/2)$\end_inset
: extra wealth is spent entirely on good 2. At \begin_inset Formula $w=1/4$\end_inset
, demand is \begin_inset Formula $(1/8,0)$\end_inset
 and all wealth is spent on good 1. The claim that quasilinear demand has no wealth effect for the non-numeraire good requires an interior numeraire choice, here \begin_inset Formula $w>1/r$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
From CES to Flexible Translog Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CES imposes one common, constant substitution elasticity. Applied demand analysis often needs substitution patterns that vary with relative prices. A translog specification uses a quadratic polynomial in logarithms, allowing such variation. Direct-utility, indirect-utility, and expenditure translog specifications are different parametric restrictions: the dual of a direct translog utility need not be translog. Chapter 2 develops a homothetic translog expenditure model, derives its demand shares, and checks the price region on which it represents a classical rational consumer. This order lets us explain the model through expenditure minimization rather than present an unexplained demand formula.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For further practice, Problems 1.2-1.5 compare these specifications. The artists case study, Problem 10.4, uses CES fan demand as the first stage of a model of streaming revenue, concert production, and ticket pricing; its consumer part can be completed now.
\end_layout

