\begin_layout Section
Expenditure Minimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

,
 the expenditure minimization problem (EMP) asks for the least expenditure required to attain utility 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{u}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e(p,\overline{u})=\min\{p\cdot x:x\in X,\ u(x)\geq\overline{u}\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a continuous utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 defined on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and the price vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(i) If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the UMP when 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the EMP when the required level of utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 the minimized expenditure level 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

 is exactly the wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(ii) If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the EMP when the required utility level is 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{u}>u(0)$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the UMP when wealth is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 the maximized utility level 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{*})=\overline{u}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proof uses local nonsatiation to show that both constraints bind and then applies the definitions of the two optimization problems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Expenditure function
\series default
.
 The function 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 is the value of the EMP.
 Whenever a solution exists,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

 for every expenditure-minimizing bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a continuous utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 defined on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and the target utility in the attainable range.
 The expenditure 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(i) Homogeneous of degree one in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(ii) Nondecreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{u}$
\end_inset

 and nondecreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

.
 Strict increase in the utility target requires restricting attention to targets above the baseline utility and conditions ensuring that the minimum reaches the target exactly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(iii) Concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(iv) Continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i) Multiplying every price by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 does not change the feasible set and multiplies the objective by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

;
 hence 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\alpha p,\overline{u})=\alpha e(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
 For (ii),
 increasing the utility target shrinks the feasible set,
 while increasing a price cannot reduce the expenditure on any fixed nonnegative bundle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iii) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\alpha}=\alpha p+(1-\alpha)p'$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 and choose any 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\alpha}\in h(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
 Feasibility of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 at each price vector gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned}e(p^{\alpha},\overline{u}) & =\alpha p\cdot x^{\alpha}+(1-\alpha)p'\cdot x^{\alpha}\\
 & \geq\alpha e(p,\overline{u})+(1-\alpha)e(p',\overline{u}).
\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset

 Hence the expenditure function is concave in prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iv) Continuity in 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuity follows from the standard expenditure-function theorem.
 The unbounded feasible set cannot be inserted directly into Berge's theorem.
 Instead,
 around any 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 with strictly positive prices,
 one first obtains a common expenditure upper bound from one feasible bundle.
 Strict positivity of nearby prices then confines every optimizer to a common compact box.
 Berge's theorem applies to this locally compactified problem.
 Continuity of preferences and local nonsatiation ensure feasibility stability and that an optimum reaches the target utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

