\begin_layout Section
Shephard's Lemma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Every Hicksian bundle is a supergradient of the concave expenditure function in prices.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\cdot,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

,
 Hicksian demand is unique there and Shephard's identity holds:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{p}e(p,\overline{u})=h(p,\overline{u}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{h}\in h(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
 For every other price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p'$
\end_inset

,
 the same bundle remains feasible,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e(p',\overline{u})\leq p'\cdot x^{h}=e(p,\overline{u})+(p'-p)\cdot x^{h}.
\]

\end_inset

 Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{h}$
\end_inset

 is a supergradient of the concave function 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\cdot,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
 At a differentiability point the superdifferential is the singleton 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\nabla_{p}e(p,\overline{u})\}$
\end_inset

,
 so every Hicksian bundle equals that gradient and demand is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
