\begin_layout Section
Integrability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now ask for sufficient conditions under which an observed demand function is generated by utility maximization.
 In the abstract choice setting,
 WARP and GARP restrict rationalizability.
 Competitive budget sets are a smaller domain than arbitrary menus,
 so differentiable demand requires additional restrictions.
 The relevant restrictions are stated in terms of the Slutsky matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume throughout this subsection that the demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that the Slutsky matrix function is 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

,
 or in the scalar version 
\begin_inset Formula $[S(p,w)]_{\ell k}=\partial_{p_{k}}x_{\ell}(p,w)+\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)x_{k}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,k\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Integrability roadmap.
 On an open domain,
 Walras' law,
 homogeneity,
 and a symmetric negative-semidefinite Slutsky matrix are the central differential restrictions.
 They are necessary for every regular utility-generated demand.
 A sufficiency theorem additionally specifies rank or nondegeneracy conditions,
 the topology of the price-wealth domain,
 and boundary behavior for the associated differential system.
 Because alternative integrability theorems impose different global hypotheses,
 this text does not present the preceding checklist as a stand-alone global theorem;
 applications must cite and verify a precise version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The qualifications are essential.
  Symmetry and negative semidefiniteness alone,
 stated on an unspecified domain,
 do not guarantee a global representation:
 one must control the domain,
 boundary behavior,
 and the differential equation's existence and uniqueness.
  Conversely,
 every continuously differentiable demand generated by a regular locally nonsatiated utility satisfies Walras' law,
 homogeneity,
 symmetry,
 and negative semidefiniteness of the Slutsky matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
Some versions of the global integrability theorem impose a bound on wealth elasticity,
 while others replace it with different domain or boundary conditions.
 No such bound is a universal consequence of local Slutsky restrictions;
 the hypotheses of the particular global theorem being used must be checked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
