\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
WGARP and Coalitional Multi-Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Afriat's theorem asks whether all observations can be generated by maximization of one utility function.
 Its answer is GARP.
 A failure of GARP rejects that joint transitive explanation,
 but it need not make every pair of observations inconsistent.
 WGARP asks the weaker pairwise question:
 can the data avoid every strict two-observation reversal even when a longer revealed-preference cycle remains?
 Coalitional multi-utility (CMU) is the optimizing model behind that condition
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "aguiarhjertstrandserranoevren2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Crucially,
 CMU extends the benchmark model:
 ordinary utility maximization is exactly its singleton special case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Budget WGARP and WARP.
 The finite dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}$
\end_inset

 satisfies WGARP if,
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t\in\mathbb{T}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{t}\cdot x^{t}\geq p^{t}\cdot x^{s}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\neg\bigl(p^{s}\cdot x^{s}>p^{s}\cdot x^{t}\bigr).
\]

\end_inset

It satisfies WARP if,
 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\neq x^{s}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{t}\cdot x^{t}\geq p^{t}\cdot x^{s}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad p^{s}\cdot x^{s}<p^{s}\cdot x^{t}.
\]

\end_inset

WGARP is the weak/strict condition appropriate for a demand correspondence.
 WARP also excludes mutual affordability at equality and corresponds to a strict,
 single-valued version of the model.
 GARP and SARP apply the analogous prohibitions after taking revealed-preference closure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Preference-function demand.
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{preference-function}{r:X\times X\to\mathbb{R}}$
\end_inset

 is a numerical comparison rule,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq_{r}y$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,y)\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Its demand correspondence is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{preference-demand}{x_{r}(p,w)}=\{x\in B(p,w):r(x,y)\geq0\text{ for every }y\in B(p,w)\}.
\]

\end_inset

The function is asymmetric when 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,y)\geq0\Rightarrow r(y,x)\leq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,y)>0\Rightarrow r(y,x)<0$
\end_inset

.
 It is strictly increasing when 
\begin_inset Formula $x>z\Rightarrow r(x,y)>r(z,y)$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 The strict part of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq_{r}$
\end_inset

 remains the relational strict part;
 it is not defined merely by 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,y)>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Coherent CMU.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $C(X)$
\end_inset

 be the continuous real-valued functions on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 with the compact-open topology.
 A compact coalition structure 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{cmu-coalition-structure}{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is a nonempty compact collection of nonempty compact sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{cmu-coalition}{U\subseteq C(X)}$
\end_inset

.
 Its preference function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{cmu-preference-function}{r_{\Omega}(x,y)}=\max_{U\in\Omega}\min_{u\in U}\bigl[u(x)-u(y)\bigr].
\]

\end_inset

The structure is coherent if 
\begin_inset Formula $U\cap V\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $U,V\in\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Coherence is pairwise intersection;
 it does not require one utility to belong to every coalition.
 A CMU comparison holds when at least one coalition unanimously ranks the first bundle at least as high as the second.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Classical utility maximization is nested exactly.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u\in C(X)$
\end_inset

 and set 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{u}=\{\{u\}\}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{u}$
\end_inset

 is compact and coherent,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\Omega_{u}}(x,y)=u(x)-u(y)$
\end_inset

,
 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{r_{\Omega_{u}}}(p,w)=\arg\max_{x\in B(p,w)}u(x).
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "prop:cmu-singleton"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Both optimization operators in the CMU formula collapse for the singleton coalition,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\Omega_{u}}(x,y)=u(x)-u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x_{r_{\Omega_{u}}}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 exactly when 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

 for every feasible 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

,
 which is the ordinary utility-maximization condition.
 A singleton is compact and intersects itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Reader theorem 
\begin_inset Formula $(\star)$
\end_inset

:
 WGARP and coherent CMU.
 For a finite expenditure-exhausting dataset with 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq2$
\end_inset

,
 strictly positive prices,
 and nonzero nonnegative choices,
 the following are equivalent:
 (i) WGARP;
 (ii) rationalization by an asymmetric,
 strictly increasing,
 continuous preference function;
 (iii) rationalization by a coherent,
 strictly increasing,
 continuous CMU;
 and (iv) rationalization by a skew-symmetric,
 coherent,
 continuous CMU whose coalitions contain strictly increasing concave utilities.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thmstar:wgarp-cmu"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is an exact finite-data characterization,
 not only a necessary condition.
 The difficult direction uses the fact that every two-observation subdataset of a WGARP dataset satisfies GARP,
 constructs a pairwise Afriat utility for each pair,
 and assembles those utilities into a coherent coalition structure.
 Passing WGARP does not identify nontransitivity:
 every GARP dataset also passes WGARP and can admit both classical and CMU rationalizations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Reader theorem 
\begin_inset Formula $(\star)$
\end_inset

:
 WARP and strict coherent CMU.
 In the same finite environment,
 WARP is equivalent to rationalization by an asymmetric,
 strictly star-concave,
 continuous preference function;
 equivalently,
 by a coherent strictly star-concave CMU;
 equivalently,
 by a skew-symmetric coherent CMU whose coalitions contain strictly increasing strictly concave utilities.
 Here strict star-concavity requires 
\begin_inset Formula $r(\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y,x)>\alpha r(x,x)+(1-\alpha)r(y,x)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thmstar:warp-cmu"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The WARP theorem is separate from the WGARP theorem.
 WARP is not the finite-data condition for maximization of one ordinary utility function:
 GARP is the classical weak-rationalizability condition,
 and SARP is the corresponding strict acyclicity condition.
 The strict CMU theorem yields at most one demanded bundle on a budget when demand is nonempty,
 but it does not guarantee nonempty demand on every counterfactual budget.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
A three-good WGARP dataset that violates GARP.
 Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
p^{1}=(4,1,5), & p^{2}=(5,4,1), & p^{3}=(1,5,4),\\
x^{1}=(4,1,1), & x^{2}=(1,4,1), & x^{3}=(1,1,4).
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[p^{t}\cdot x^{s}]^{3}_{t,s=1}=\begin{pmatrix}22 & 13 & 25\\
25 & 22 & 13\\
13 & 25 & 22
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset

Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{1}\succ^{R,D}x^{2}\succ^{R,D}x^{3}\succ^{R,D}x^{1}$
\end_inset

,
 so GARP fails.
 Each reverse-budget cost is 
\begin_inset Formula $25>22$
\end_inset

,
 however,
 so no pair creates a WGARP reversal.
 This dataset also satisfies WARP because the chosen bundles are distinct and no pair is mutually affordable.
 With two goods,
 WGARP and GARP coincide;
 three goods are essential for this separation under linear budgets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Compensated law of demand for preference functions.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 be asymmetric and strictly increasing,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x_{r}(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 and set 
\begin_inset Formula $w'=p'\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x'\in x_{r}(p',w')$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p'-p)\cdot(x'-x)\leq0.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "prop:cmu-cld"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
First note that strict increase and asymmetry imply budget exhaustion at every nonempty demand.
 If a demanded bundle had slack,
 a strictly larger feasible bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 would satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,z)\geq0$
\end_inset

 by maximality,
 while strict increase in the first argument would give 
\begin_inset Formula $r(z,z)>r(x,z)\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting asymmetry at 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,z)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $p'\cdot(x'-x)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose,
 toward a contradiction,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x'<p\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 Then some 
\begin_inset Formula $z>x'$
\end_inset

 remains affordable at the original budget.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x'$
\end_inset

 defeats 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at the compensated budget,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x',x)\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Strict increase gives 
\begin_inset Formula $r(z,x)>0$
\end_inset

,
 and asymmetry then gives 
\begin_inset Formula $r(x,z)<0$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x_{r}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot(x'-x)\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 and subtracting this inequality from 
\begin_inset Formula $p'\cdot(x'-x)=0$
\end_inset

 yields the result.
 The claim is conditional on both demand sets being nonempty.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
What survives,
 and what does not.
 Singleton CMU reproduces the UMP exactly.
 Under asymmetry and strict increase,
 nonempty CMU demand satisfies budget exhaustion,
 homogeneity of degree zero,
 WGARP,
 and the compensated inequality above.
 WGARP or coherence alone does not imply one scalar utility,
 transitivity,
 GARP,
 convex preferences,
 nonempty counterfactual demand,
 Roy's identity,
 Shephard's lemma,
 Slutsky symmetry,
 or the standard scalar-utility welfare measures.
 Concavity of the component utilities does not make the induced CMU relation globally convex.
 An arbitrary noncoherent CMU need not satisfy WGARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The star marks a compressed reader theorem,
 not a weaker truth standard and not a Lean-verification badge.
 The six-way WGARP statement,
 the distinct WARP theorem,
 topology,
 proof boundary,
 and formalization record appear in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "app:cmu-technical"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

