\begin_layout Section
SARP and Strict Rationalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The preceding section used GARP to characterize weak rationalization,
 allowing ties in observed choice.
 SARP is the acyclicity condition associated with strict or unique rationalization.
 The exact equivalence depends on how duplicate observed bundles and strict choice are treated,
 so this section states the condition without claiming that merely replacing every Afriat inequality by a strict one is sufficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
(Strong axiom of revealed preference,
 SARP.) The dataset satisfies SARP if the reflexive-transitive revealed relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{R}$
\end_inset

 has no nontrivial cycle:
 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\succeq^{R}x^{s}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\neq x^{s}$
\end_inset

,
 it is not the case that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{s}\succeq^{R}x^{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
SARP is the natural condition when observed choice is single-valued and ties are ruled out.
 The weak Afriat theorem above is the maintained finite-data result in this book;
 strict rationalization results require additional assumptions,
 including care with duplicate observed bundles.
\end_layout

