\begin_layout Section
Experimental Tests of GARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Choi,
 Fisman,
 Gale,
 and Kariv study 93 participants recruited from undergraduate classes and staff at the University of California,
 Berkeley.
 Each participant completed 50 portfolio-allocation problems.
 In every problem the participant chose a point on a linear budget over two Arrow securities,
 one paying in each of two states.
 A chosen portfolio can therefore be interpreted as a bundle of state-contingent consumption.
 The 47 participants in the symmetric treatment faced 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}=\pi_{2}=1/2$
\end_inset

;
 the remaining 46 faced 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\pi_{1},\pi_{2}\}=\{1/3,2/3\}$
\end_inset

.
 The computer varied the budget line across rounds,
 and each line may be normalized as 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\cdot x=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 generated a panel 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}_{i}=\{(p^{t}_{i},x^{t}_{i})\}^{50}_{t=1}$
\end_inset

 to which GARP can be applied
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "choifismangalekariv2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

