\begin_layout Section
Measurement Error and Statistical Revealed Preference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ahn,
 Choi,
 Gale,
 and Kariv collect 50 portfolio choices from each of 154 participants.
 The assets are three Arrow securities corresponding to three states:
 one state has a known probability,
 whereas the other two states are ambiguous.
 This is a three-state contingent-consumption experiment,
 not an ordinary three-good demand experiment.
 Applied directly to the recorded choices and budgets,
 the deterministic GARP test is passed by 12.99 percent of participants
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "ahnchoigalekariv2014,aguiarkashaev2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A deterministic revealed-preference test treats recorded prices and choices as the economically relevant data.
 Aguiar and Kashaev instead ask whether the population distribution of recorded panels can be generated by latent utility-maximizing behavior together with a disciplined measurement process
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "aguiarkashaev2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For participant 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and task 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{t}_{i},x^{t}_{i})$
\end_inset

 denote the recorded pair and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*t}_{i},x^{*t}_{i})$
\end_inset

 the latent pair entering the maintained optimizing model.
 An additive relation such as 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}_{i}=x^{*t}_{i}+\varepsilon^{t}_{x,i}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}_{i}=p^{*t}_{i}+\varepsilon^{t}_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 is a schematic illustration,
 not the identifying content of the framework.
 Empirical content comes from restrictions linking observed and latent variables---especially population centering conditions---combined with utility-maximization restrictions.
 If both latent data and measurement error are unrestricted,
 observed violations can be rationalized too freely for the exercise to be informative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In their application,
 Aguiar and Kashaev study two measurement channels separately.
 Under the price-misperception specification,
 recorded choices are exact and perceived prices may differ from displayed prices,
 subject to agreement between perceived and displayed price vectors on average for each task.
 Under the trembling-hand specification,
 displayed prices are exact and chosen allocations may contain mean-zero error.
 The validation evidence is asymmetric:
 the paper reports direct evidence for the centering condition on hand errors,
 whereas the evidence for price misperception is indirect and tied to the graphical design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empirical comparison is therefore conditional.
 At the 5 percent significance level,
 the authors do not reject the joint null of static utility maximization and mean-centered price misperception.
 They strongly reject the joint null when the only discrepancy is a centered trembling-hand error in consumption.
 The data thus favor the price-misperception specification over that particular consumption-error specification.
 They do not establish that every participant is deterministically GARP-consistent,
 that price misperception is the unique source of every violation,
 or that utility maximization has been confirmed.
 Failure to reject is evidence about a joint model of preferences and measurement,
 not proof of rationality.
\end_layout

