\begin_layout Section
From Choice Data to Preference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The preceding chapters used the geometry of linear budgets.
  We now abstract from prices and wealth and retain only alternatives,
 feasible menus,
 and observed choices.
  This separation shows which conclusions follow from choice consistency itself and which depend on consumer-budget structure.
  It also supplies the preference and utility language that underlies the benchmark model used in Chapter 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:preference-and-choice:02:choice-environments
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Choice Environments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a nonempty set of alternatives and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}\subseteq2^{X}\setminus\{\emptyset\}$
\end_inset

 be the family of feasible menus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Choice correspondence.
 A choice correspondence is a map 
\begin_inset Formula $c:\mathcal{A}\rightrightarrows X$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\neq c(A)\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 A choice structure is the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Exact rationalization.
 A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 rationalizes 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $c(A)=\{x\in A:x\succeq y\text{ for every }y\in A\}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 A rational preference relation is complete and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Replacing equality by the inclusion 
\begin_inset Formula $c(A)\subseteq\{x\in A:x\succeq y\text{ for every }y\in A\}$
\end_inset

 is only a selection-from-maximizers condition.
 By itself it has no revealed-preference content:
 universal indifference makes every feasible alternative greatest and therefore accommodates every nonempty choice correspondence.
 Whenever the book uses weak rationalization for budget data,
 it instead imposes the structural conditions stated in Afriat's theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:preference-and-choice:03:preference-relations
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Preference Relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
For a relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

,
 define its strict and symmetric parts by 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y\iff x\succeq y\text{ and not }y\succeq x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y\iff x\succeq y\text{ and }y\succeq x$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

 is irreflexive and transitive,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation.
 These elementary implications are the order-theoretic basis for utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Finite utility representation.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is finite,
 a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 has a real-valued utility representation if and only if it is complete and transitive.
 A representation is unique up to a strictly increasing transformation of its range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an infinite commodity or choice space,
 representation requires additional assumptions such as continuity and a suitable countability condition.
 The next theorem gives the continuous-utility result for the Euclidean commodity space used in Chapter 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:consumer-theory-utility-maximization:02:utility-representation
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Utility Representation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is continuous if its upper and lower contour sets are closed:
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\{x\in X:x\succeq y\}\quad\text{and}\quad\{x\in X:y\succeq x\}
\]

\end_inset

are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\to x$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\succeq y$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succeq x^{n}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succeq x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
Every total preorder on a countable set has a real-valued utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{\succeq}$
\end_inset

 be the symmetric part of the preorder.
 The quotient 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{quotient-set}{X/I_{\succeq}}$
\end_inset

 is countable,
 and the relation induced on it is a linear order by Appendix A.
 Every countable linear order admits an order embedding into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Q}$
\end_inset

:
 enumerate its elements and insert the image of each new element at a rational number between the images of its already embedded lower and upper neighbors.
 Composing this embedding with the quotient map gives 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(Continuous utility representation) If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is a continuous total preorder on 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
 then there exists a continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in X$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x\succeq y\iff u(x)\geq u(y).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Proof record.
 This is Debreu's continuous-utility theorem specialized to the Euclidean orthant;
 see
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "debreu1959"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The orthant is second countable,
 separable,
 and connected,
 and a continuous total preorder on this space satisfies the theorem's topological hypotheses.
 The published proof constructs an order-dense countable family of indifference classes,
 assigns compatible real values to those classes,
 and extends the assignment continuously to the whole space.
 We rely on that theorem here rather than reproduce its topological construction.
 An arbitrary limit of a utility defined only on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Q}^{L}$
\end_inset

 would not suffice,
 because existence and independence of the approximating sequence would still have to be proved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The theorem guarantees existence,
 not uniqueness.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing on the range of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents the same preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:preference-and-choice:04:revealed-choice-and-wgarp
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Revealed Choice and WGARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Direct revealed preference.
 For a choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

,
 write 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notationfirst{choice-direct-revealed}{\succeq^{C}}y$
\end_inset

 if there is a menu 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in c(A)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

.
 Write 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notationfirst{choice-strict-revealed}{\succ^{C}}y$
\end_inset

 if,
 in addition,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y\notin c(A)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{choice-revealed-closure}{\succeq^{C*}}$
\end_inset

 be the reflexive-transitive closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Weak Generalized Axiom of Revealed Preference (WGARP).
 The choice correspondence satisfies WGARP when 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq^{C}y$
\end_inset

 implies not 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ^{C}x$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently,
 if two menus contain both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

,
 choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 from one and strictly choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 from the other is ruled out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Necessity of WGARP.
 If a complete and transitive preference relation rationalizes a choice structure,
 then the structure satisfies WGARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in c(A)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

,
 rationalization gives 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset

.
 If a second menu strictly chose 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it would give 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting the first comparison.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
ECON803_CONSUMER_RESTRUCTURE_SOURCE:section:preference-and-choice:05:garp-on-finite-data
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
GARP in Abstract Choice and the Budget-Data Specialization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Generalized Axiom of Revealed Preference (GARP).
 A finite dataset satisfies GARP when 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq^{C*}y$
\end_inset

 never occurs together with 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ^{C}x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus an indirect revealed-preference chain may return to an alternative only when the return comparison is not strict.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Afriat preview.
 On finite linear-budget data with positive prices and expenditure exhaustion,
 GARP is equivalent to rationalization by a continuous,
 strictly increasing,
 concave utility function.
 Chapter 3 states the exact theorem,
 writes the Afriat inequalities,
 and constructs the rationalizing utility
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "afriat1967,varian1982"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This is a result about budget geometry and locally nonsatiated utility,
 not an equivalence with unrestricted selection from the greatest elements of an arbitrary relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WGARP is the one-step restriction for a possibly multivalued choice correspondence.
 GARP applies the no-strict-reversal requirement to the reflexive-transitive closure of direct revealed preference and is therefore the relevant condition when the data do not contain every pair of alternatives in a common menu.
 Exact single-valued rationalization can require additional tie-breaking assumptions,
 so the theorem above is stated for weak rationalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{a,b,c\}$
\end_inset

 and suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $a\succ^{C}b$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b\succ^{C}c$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\succ^{C}a$
\end_inset

.
 This strict cycle violates GARP and cannot be generated by a transitive preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The separation between the abstract choice language and the budget-set language is deliberate.
 In Chapter 3,
 prices and wealth impose the additional linear-budget structure,
 and the Afriat inequalities provide a finite certificate that can be checked mechanically.
\end_layout

