\begin_layout Chapter
Expected Utility and Risk
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:uncertainty"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expected utility adds two ingredients to the choice framework:
 objective lotteries and a mixture operation.
 The chapter first gives the finite von Neumann--Morgenstern model and its affine uniqueness,
 then studies risk attitudes,
 stochastic dominance,
 portfolio choice,
 and revealed tests.
 Infinite lottery spaces are treated last because their topology and integrability assumptions are technical extensions rather than part of the finite core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Primitives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{prize-set}Z\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 be a finite set of monetary prizes.
  A lottery is a probability mass function 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{lottery}{\mu}:Z\to[0,1]$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{z\in Z}\mu(z)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Write 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{lottery-space}{\Delta(Z)}$
\end_inset

 for the set of lotteries and 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{degenerate-lottery}{\delta_{z}}$
\end_inset

 for the degenerate lottery assigning probability one to 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Mixtures are defined pointwise by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[\alpha\mu+(1-\alpha)\nu](z)=\alpha\mu(z)+(1-\alpha)\nu(z),\qquad\alpha\in[0,1].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a Bernoulli utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
 expected utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{expected-utility}{U(\mu)}=\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\mu(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Expected-Utility Representation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We observe a choice correspondence on a family of subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 and represent the decision maker's preference by a relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 on lotteries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Expected utility representation.
 The preference relation has an expected utility representation if there is a Bernoulli utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\succeq\nu\iff\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\mu(z)\geq\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\nu(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Independence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume throughout this section that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Independence Axiom) For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu,\nu,\eta\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\succeq\nu$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\mu+(1-\alpha)\eta\succeq\alpha\nu+(1-\alpha)\eta.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other axiom that we need is more technical
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Mixture continuity) For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu,\nu,\eta\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\succ\nu\succ\eta$
\end_inset

,
 then the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha\in[0,1]|\alpha\mu+(1-\alpha)\eta\succ\nu\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\alpha\in[0,1]|\nu\succ\alpha\mu+(1-\alpha)\eta\}$
\end_inset

 are open in 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Finite Expected-Utility Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
The rational preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Independence Axiom and Mixture continuity if and only if there exists a (Bernoulli) utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\succeq\nu$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\mu(z)\geq\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\nu(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First we prove that there is an affine representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
If the preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Independence Axiom and mixture continuity then there is an affine function 
\begin_inset Formula $U:\Delta(Z)\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 that represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Choose best and worst degenerate lotteries 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{+}=\delta_{z^{+}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{-}=\delta_{z^{-}}$
\end_inset

 among the finitely many prizes.
 Repeated use of independence shows 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{+}\succeq\mu\succeq\mu^{-}$
\end_inset

 for every lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{+}\sim\mu^{-}$
\end_inset

,
 all lotteries are indifferent and a constant affine representation works.
 Otherwise,
 independence implies 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta\mu^{+}+(1-\beta)\mu^{-}\succ\alpha\mu^{+}+(1-\alpha)\mu^{-}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\beta>\alpha.
\]

\end_inset

For a fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

,
 mixture continuity makes the two sets of mixture weights giving an outcome weakly above or weakly below 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 closed.
 They cover the connected interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

 and contain opposite endpoints,
 so they intersect.
 Strict monotonicity along the best--worst segment gives a unique 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{\mu}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu\sim\alpha_{\mu}\mu^{+}+(1-\alpha_{\mu})\mu^{-}.
\]

\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\mu)=\alpha_{\mu}$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 independence permits substitution of the equivalent best--worst mixtures for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

,
 yielding 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(\beta\mu+(1-\beta)\nu)=\beta U(\mu)+(1-\beta)U(\nu).
\]

\end_inset

Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is affine on the lottery simplex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second step is to show that an affine representation on the lottery simplex has the expected-utility form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $U:\Delta(Z)\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is affine if and only if there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $u:Z\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\mu)=\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\mu(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For finite 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

,
 no functional-analytic representation theorem is needed.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $u(z)=U(\delta_{z})$
\end_inset

.
 Since every lottery has the finite decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\sum_{z\in Z}\mu(z)\delta_{z}$
\end_inset

,
 affinity of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\mu)=\sum_{z\in Z}u(z)\mu(z)$
\end_inset

.
 The converse follows immediately from distributivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=\{0,10\}$
\end_inset

,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

 satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(0)=\mu(10)=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\mu)=\frac{1}{2}u(10)+\frac{1}{2}u(0)$
\end_inset

.
 Normalize the Bernoulli index by 
\begin_inset Formula $u(0)=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u(10)=1$
\end_inset

.
 The normalized expected utility is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\mu)=1/2$
\end_inset

.
 Any positive affine transformation of this index represents the same preference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Affine Uniqueness and Recoverability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $U:\Delta(Z)\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U':\Delta(Z)\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 be affine maps that represent 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

,
 then there exist real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $U'(\mu)=\alpha U(\mu)+b$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u,v$
\end_inset

 are Bernoulli utilities such that 
\begin_inset Formula $u\cdot\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v\cdot\mu$
\end_inset

 represent 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 then there are real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $v\cdot\mu=\alpha u\cdot\mu+b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Affine uniqueness is stronger than ordinal uniqueness in deterministic choice.
 For an ordinary utility representation,
 every strictly increasing transformation preserves the ranking.
 Under expected utility,
 mixture linearity restricts admissible transformations to positive affine maps;
 a nonlinear increasing transformation generally destroys the independence representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Allais Paradox
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=\{z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}\}$
\end_inset

 with the ordered coordinate list 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{1},z_{2},z_{3})=(2.5,0.5,0)$
\end_inset

 million dollars and 
\begin_inset Formula $N:=|Z|=3$
\end_inset

.
  A lottery vector lists probabilities in this order,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{z}:=u(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

