\begin_layout Chapter
Stochastic Choice and Random Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:random-utility"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Deterministic choice predicts a set of maximizers from each menu;
 stochastic choice records a probability distribution over that menu.
 We first define a coherent choice-probability system on a fixed finite universe,
 then ask when it is a mixture of deterministic rankings.
 Multinomial logit is presented afterward as a parametric special case,
 not as the definition of random utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Stochastic Choice on a Finite Universe
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be a finite universe of alternatives and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}=2^{Y}\setminus\{\varnothing\}$
\end_inset

 be its collection of nonempty menus.
 A stochastic choice rule is a family 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{stochastic-choice}{P=\{P_{B}\}_{B\in\mathcal{B}}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{B}\in\Delta(B)$
\end_inset

 for every menu.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{B}(a)\geq0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in B$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{a\in B}P_{B}(a)=1$
\end_inset

,
 and unavailable alternatives receive probability zero.
 The family,
 rather than a probability from only one menu,
 is the empirical object to be rationalized.
 A deterministic choice function is the special case in which every 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{B}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Random Utility Representation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Random-utility representation.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be the finite universe,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{random-utility-space}{(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})}$
\end_inset

 be a probability space,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{random-utility}{\boldsymbol{U}}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^{Y}$
\end_inset

 be a measurable random utility vector.
 For every nonempty menu 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq Y$
\end_inset

,
 the induced choice probabilities are 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{B}(a)=\mathbb{P}\{\boldsymbol{U}(a)>\boldsymbol{U}(b)\text{ for every }b\in B\setminus\{a\}\}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume either that ties occur with probability zero or that a measurable tie-breaking rule is specified;
 under the zero-tie convention the displayed strict-max probabilities sum to one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When choice probabilities are observed on every nonempty menu,
 the deterministic types form the finite set 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{order-types}{\mathcal{R}_{Y}}$
\end_inset

 of strict linear orders on 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

.
 Stacking the probabilities gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}=M\lambda$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\Delta(\mathcal{R}_{Y})$
\end_inset

 is a distribution over those orders and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{(a,B),r}=\mathbb{I}\{a\text{ is }r\text{-best in }B\}$
\end_inset

.
 The finite feasibility condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}=M\lambda$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{1}^{\top}_{|\mathcal{R}_{Y}|}\lambda=1$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to the existence of such a distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A Parametric Discrete-Choice Specification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Index alternatives by 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\{0,1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $j=0$
\end_inset

 denoting an outside option,
 and consumers by 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 be observed attributes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

 price,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 income,
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{j}$
\end_inset

 systematic utility,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 an unobserved preference shock.
 Conditional indirect utility is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{latent-utility}{U^{*}_{ij}}=V_{j}(x_{j},p_{j},y_{i})+\varepsilon_{ij}.
\]

\end_inset

The corresponding probability is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{choice-probability}{P_{ij}}=\Pr\{U^{*}_{ij}\geq U^{*}_{ik}\text{ for every available }k\}.
\]

\end_inset

Ties occur with probability zero if every pairwise utility difference is atomless;
 a joint shock density is a familiar sufficient condition.
 A continuous marginal distribution for each shock alone is not sufficient when shocks may be dependent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the outside good has price 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}$
\end_inset

 and quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

,
 the budget identity 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}+p_{0}z=y_{i}$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $z=(y_{i}-p_{j})/p_{0}$
\end_inset

 after alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is purchased.
 This reduction is valid only when the implied outside-good quantity is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Multinomial Logit and IIA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose the shocks are independent and identically distributed Type-I extreme value with scale 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{logit-scale}{\tau}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then the multinomial-logit probabilities are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{ij}=\frac{\exp(V_{j}/\tau)}{\sum^{J}_{k=0}\exp(V_{k}/\tau)}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Independence of irrelevant alternatives.
 Under multinomial logit,
 for any two alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $j,k$
\end_inset

 that remain available,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{P_{ij}}{P_{ik}}=\exp((V_{j}-V_{k})/\tau)$
\end_inset

 is independent of the other alternatives in the menu.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Red-bus/blue-bus.
 If walking and a red bus initially have equal probabilities,
 adding a blue bus that is nearly identical to the red bus should split the bus share while leaving walking's share close to its original value.
 Multinomial logit instead preserves the walking-to-red-bus odds ratio,
 illustrating the empirical content and limitation of IIA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The multinomial probit model replaces the extreme-value shocks with a jointly normal vector.
 Its pairwise differences are generally correlated,
 so it does not impose IIA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Random Expected Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Random expected utility.
 For a finite prize set 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 and lotteries 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset

,
 a random Bernoulli utility 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{u}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^{Z}$
\end_inset

 induces 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{U}_{\omega}(\mu)=\sum_{z\in Z}\boldsymbol{u}_{\omega}(z)\mu(z)$
\end_inset

.
 Choice probabilities are obtained by maximizing this random expected utility on each menu of lotteries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 The representation imposes linearity in mixtures and regularity under menu expansion.
 Extremeness and weak-star continuity are additional conditions in the Gul--Pesendorfer characterization;
 see
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gulpesendorfer2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Attributes,
 Choice Probabilities,
 and Consumer Surplus
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With observed attributes 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x_{a})_{a\in A}$
\end_inset

,
 define the mean-utility vector 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x)=(v(a,x_{a}))_{a\in A}\in\mathbb{R}^{A}$
\end_inset

.
 A common specification is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(a,x)=v(a,x_{a})+\varepsilon_{a},
\]

\end_inset

 where the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_{a})_{a\in A}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 For a 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional linear-index model,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{a},\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and write 
\begin_inset Formula $v(a,x_{a})=x^{\top}_{a}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Independence is a maintained exogeneity assumption,
 not a consequence of the random-utility definition.
 For any mean-utility vector 
\begin_inset Formula $v=(v(a))_{a\in A}\in\mathbb{R}^{A}$
\end_inset

,
 define the conditional choice probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{A}(a\mid v):=\mathbb{P}\{v(a)+\varepsilon_{a}>v(b)+\varepsilon_{b}\text{ for every }b\in A\setminus\{a\}\}$
\end_inset

 under the same zero-tie convention as above.
 Conditional on attributes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{A}(a\mid x)$
\end_inset

 is shorthand for 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{A}(a\mid v(x))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Expected maximum utility.
 The expected value as a function of the mean-utility vector is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{expected-maximum}{\mathcal{V}(v)}=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\max_{a\in A}\{v(a)+\varepsilon_{a}\}\right],\qquad v\in\mathbb{R}^{A}.
\]

\end_inset

 If the deterministic utility contains 
\begin_inset Formula $-\alpha p_{a}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 as the price coefficient,
 the publication-safe money-metric object is the change between two scenarios,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta CS=[\mathcal{V}(v^{1})-\mathcal{V}(v^{0})]/\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 The level 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}(v)/\alpha$
\end_inset

 depends on the additive utility normalization.
 If shocks are merely decision errors rather than preference shocks,
 this expression is not a welfare measure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Williams--Daly--Zachary envelope result.
 See
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "mcfadden1981"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 If the shock distribution has finite first moments and ties occur with probability zero,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}$
\end_inset

 is convex and differentiable in the vector of mean utilities,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\mathcal{V}(v)}{\partial v(a)}=P_{A}(a\mid v)$
\end_inset

.
 If choice probabilities are differentiable in mean utilities (equivalently,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}$
\end_inset

 is twice differentiable at the point),
 then the Jacobian of choice probabilities is the Hessian of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}$
\end_inset

,
 hence symmetric and positive semidefinite.
 Translation invariance implies that the rows and columns sum to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The envelope identity is the basis for likelihood and welfare calculations in discrete-choice models.
 Additional sign restrictions such as monotone substitution patterns require additional assumptions on the shock distribution and utility index;
 they do not follow from random utility alone.
\end_layout


