Aggregation and Markets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Aggregate Demand and Representative Consumers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:aggregation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Three Questions About Aggregation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When does aggregate demand depend on prices and total wealth rather than on the entire wealth distribution?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When does aggregate demand satisfy a demand restriction such as WARP?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When can aggregate demand be given a welfare interpretation?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let consumers be indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has Marshallian demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p,w_{i})$
\end_inset

 at common prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Aggregate demand is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{aggregate-demand}{x^{A}(p,\mathbf{w})}=\sum^{I}_{i=1}x_{i}(p,w_{i}),
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{wealth-distribution}{\mathbf{w}}=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{I})$
\end_inset

.
 Total wealth is 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{total-wealth}W=\sum_{i}w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Without additional structure,
 two wealth distributions with the same 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 can generate different aggregate bundles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Gorman Aggregation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Gorman form.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has Gorman-form indirect utility if there are a consumer-specific intercept and a common positive wealth coefficient,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{gorman-form}{a_{i}(p),b(p)}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $b(p)>0$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}(p,w_{i})=a_{i}(p)+b(p)w_{i}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Gorman aggregation.
 See
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gorman1953,mwg1995"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Suppose each 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 has the Gorman form and is differentiable.
 Roy's identity gives 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}(p,w_{i})=-\frac{\nabla_{p}a_{i}(p)+w_{i}\nabla_{p}b(p)}{b(p)}.
\]

\end_inset

 Therefore aggregate demand takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{A}(p,\mathbf{w})=A(p)+B(p)W,
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $A(p)=-\sum_{i}\nabla_{p}a_{i}(p)/b(p)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p)=-\nabla_{p}b(p)/b(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Aggregate demand consequently depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,W)$
\end_inset

 and not on the distribution of wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating the displayed Gorman form yields the common wealth derivative 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{w_{i}}x_{i}(p,w_{i})=B(p)\qquad\text{for every consumer }i.
\]

\end_inset

 This equality is necessary for the displayed Gorman representation;
 no global converse is asserted without separate integrability and domain hypotheses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Aggregate Demand and WARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even if every individual demand satisfies WARP,
 aggregate demand need not do so.
 Aggregate WARP compares two price--total-wealth pairs while holding fixed the rule that assigns total wealth across consumers;
 the corresponding compensation need not leave each consumer's wealth unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix a wealth-allocation rule 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}(p,W)\in\mathbb{R}^{I}_{+}$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}w_{i}(p,W)=W$
\end_inset

,
 and define the induced price--total-wealth demand by 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W):=x^{A}(p,\mathbf{w}(p,W))$
\end_inset

.
  Every use of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W)$
\end_inset

 below holds this rule fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Aggregate WARP.
 A single-valued aggregate demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if,
 for distinct observations 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,W)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p',W')$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{A}(p',W')\leq W$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $p'\cdot x^{A}(p,W)>W'$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W)\neq x^{A}(p',W')$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Sufficient condition for aggregate WARP.
 Suppose wealth shares are fixed and strictly positive,
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=\alpha_{i}W$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}\alpha_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose every individual demand is single-valued,
 homogeneous of degree zero,
 and satisfies Walras' law.
 Assume also that,
 for every normalized price pair 
\begin_inset Formula $q,q'\gg0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(q-q')\cdot\bigl[x_{i}(q,\alpha_{i})-x_{i}(q',\alpha_{i})\bigr]\leq0,
\]

\end_inset

with strict inequality whenever the two individual demanded bundles differ.
 Then the aggregate demand formed with these fixed shares satisfies aggregate WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Set 
\begin_inset Formula $q=p/W$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X(q)=\sum_{i}x_{i}(q,\alpha_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Homogeneity gives 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W)=X(q)$
\end_inset

,
 while Walras' law gives 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot X(q)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Summing the individual inequalities yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(q-q')\cdot[X(q)-X(q')]\leq0,
\]

\end_inset

and the inequality is strict whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $X(q)\neq X(q')$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot X(q')\leq1$
\end_inset

 and the aggregate bundles differ,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0>(q-q')\cdot[X(q)-X(q')]=2-q\cdot X(q')-q'\cdot X(q),
\]

\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $q'\cdot X(q)>2-q\cdot X(q')\geq1$
\end_inset

.
 This is aggregate WARP on the normalized domain and hence on the original price--wealth domain.
 Fixed shares are essential:
 if shares change with prices or after compensation,
 the summation argument does not apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Representative Consumers and Welfare
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Positive representative consumer.
 A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{rep}$
\end_inset

 is a positive representative consumer for 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}$
\end_inset

 if its Walrasian demand equals 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}(p,W)$
\end_inset

 for every price--wealth pair in the domain.
 This is a statement about demand representation,
 not about welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Normative representative consumer.
 Fix a social welfare function 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{social-welfare}{\mathcal{W}}:\mathbb{R}^{I}\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and individual indirect utilities 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 A nonnegative wealth allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}(p,W)$
\end_inset

 is welfare-optimal if it solves 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{I}_{+}}\mathcal{W}\bigl(v_{1}(p,w_{1}),\ldots,v_{I}(p,w_{I})\bigr)\quad\text{subject to}\quad\sum_{i}w_{i}\leq W.
\]

\end_inset

.
 A positive representative consumer is normative relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{W}$
\end_inset

 only when the wealth rule used to form 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}$
\end_inset

 is welfare-optimal at every 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,W)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gorman aggregation can deliver a positive representative consumer,
 but it does not select a social welfare function or justify interpersonal utility comparisons.
 Normative claims require the welfare criterion and the wealth-allocation rule to be stated explicitly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Many-Product Consumer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fixed commodity vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is exact when the list of goods is fixed.
 It becomes a poor organizing device when products enter continuously,
 differ along many characteristics,
 and are filtered by search or recommendation before a consumer sees them.
 Amazon's current public description,
 for example,
 reports more than 300 million products across more than 35 categories
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "amazonstore2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Exact catalog counts change;
 the economic point is the scale,
 not a claim that the count is one billion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let characteristics live in a compact metric product space 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{product-space}{(\mathcal{G},d)}$
\end_inset

.
 Three objects must remain distinct:
 the available catalog 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}_{0}\subseteq\mathcal{G}$
\end_inset

,
 consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's consideration set 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}\subseteq\mathcal{G}_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 and the purchased support 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{supp}x_{i}\subseteq A_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Availability is therefore not attention,
 and attention is not positive demand.
 At the individual level,
 an unseen option and a disliked option generate the same zero purchase
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "abaluckadamsprassl2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 At the market level,
 an available product can receive zero observed empirical share,
 requiring methods that do not treat zero share as proof of unavailability
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gandhilushi2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
One item: ideal points and local diversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To isolate product-space geometry,
 suppose every consumer buys exactly one item,
 the ideal-point distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is a probability measure,
 and all products receive full consideration:
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}=\mathcal{G}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Participation can instead be modeled by adding an outside option.
 Product 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is located at 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{j}\in\mathcal{G}$
\end_inset

,
 has quality intercept 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 and price 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 and consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has ideal point 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{ideal-point}{b_{i}}\in\mathcal{G}$
\end_inset

.
 A quadratic mismatch specification is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U^{*}_{ij}=\delta_{j}-p_{j}-\kappa_{d}d(b_{i},g_{j})^{2},\qquad \kappa_{d}>0.
\]

\end_inset

The types choosing product 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 form the cell
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{product-cell}{C_{j}(p)}=
\left\{b\in\mathcal{G}:\delta_{j}-p_{j}-\kappa_{d}d(b,g_{j})^{2}
\geq \delta_{k}-p_{k}-\kappa_{d}d(b,g_{k})^{2}\ \text{for every }k\right\}.
\]

\end_inset

Demand share is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}(p)=\mu(C_{j}(p))$
\end_inset

 for the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 of ideal points.
 Assume utility-tie sets have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

-measure zero;
 otherwise fix a tie-breaking rule and define the cells accordingly.
 These are generalized weighted Voronoi cells;
 with squared Euclidean distance they are power cells.
 Equal prices and qualities imply nearest-product choice.
 With unequal prices or qualities,
 the nearest product need not be chosen.
 This formulation joins spatial competition,
 the characteristics approach,
 and discrete choice
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hotelling1929,lancaster1966,andersondepalmathisse1989,mcfadden1981"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Local diversion on a line.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}=[\underline g,\overline g]\subset\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 with Euclidean metric 
\begin_inset Formula $d(b,g)=|b-g|$
\end_inset

,
 let active products satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{1}<\cdots<g_{J}$
\end_inset

,
 and let ideal points have distribution function 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 with continuous density 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 that is strictly positive at the active boundaries.
 Fix an interior product 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and an open neighborhood of prices in which its nonempty choice interval has only products 
\begin_inset Formula $j-1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j+1$
\end_inset

 as active neighbors,
 all other alternatives remain strictly slack at its boundaries,
 and no boundary has a triple tie.
 The boundary between 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j+1$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\overline b_{j,j+1}
=\frac{g_{j}+g_{j+1}}{2}
+\frac{\delta_{j}-\delta_{j+1}-p_{j}+p_{j+1}}
{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j+1}-g_{j})},
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{j}=F(\overline b_{j,j+1})-F(\overline b_{j-1,j}).
\]

\end_inset

At every price vector preserving this active adjacency,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial s_{j}}{\partial p_{j+1}}
&=\frac{f(\overline b_{j,j+1})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j+1}-g_{j})}>0,\\
\frac{\partial s_{j}}{\partial p_{j-1}}
&=\frac{f(\overline b_{j-1,j})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j}-g_{j-1})}>0,\\
\frac{\partial s_{j}}{\partial p_{j}}
&=-\frac{f(\overline b_{j,j+1})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j+1}-g_{j})}
-\frac{f(\overline b_{j-1,j})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j}-g_{j-1})}<0,
\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset

whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial s_{j}/\partial p_{k}=0$
\end_inset

 locally for every nonneighbor 
\begin_inset Formula $k\notin\{j-1,j,j+1\}$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently,
 an increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

 diverts the lost share only to the two active neighbors:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial s_{j+1}}{\partial p_{j}}
=\frac{f(\overline b_{j,j+1})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j+1}-g_{j})},
\qquad
\frac{\partial s_{j-1}}{\partial p_{j}}
=\frac{f(\overline b_{j-1,j})}{2\kappa_{d}(g_{j}-g_{j-1})},
\]

\end_inset

all other product shares are locally unchanged,
 and these two gains sum to 
\begin_inset Formula $-\partial s_{j}/\partial p_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Equating the two adjacent utilities and expanding the squares gives the displayed boundary.
 Under stable adjacency,
 product 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's cell is exactly the interval between its left and right boundaries,
 so its share is the difference of the two distribution-function values.
 Differentiate the right boundary with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j+1}$
\end_inset

,
 and the left boundary with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 A nonneighbor's price enters neither active boundary while the cell adjacency is unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proposition is local and conditional:
 a sufficiently large price change can alter the active-neighbor graph.
 It also shows why a product metric alone is insufficient.
 The utility specification and stable cell geometry create local diversion.
 By contrast,
 conditional logit without an outside option and with price coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{p}>0$
\end_inset

 satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{j}=\frac{\exp(\delta_{j}-\alpha_{p}p_{j})}
{\sum_{k}\exp(\delta_{k}-\alpha_{p}p_{k})},
\qquad
\frac{\partial s_{j}}{\partial p_{k}}=\alpha_{p}s_{j}s_{k}>0\quad(j\neq k),
\]

\end_inset

so every product substitutes for every other product regardless of distance.
 Random coefficients allow richer substitution,
 but distance decay is still a restriction to be modeled and tested rather than a theorem of discrete choice
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "berrylevinsohnpakes1995,berrypakes2007,feenstralevinsohn1995,pinksesladebrett2002"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Many items: bundles over product space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A continuum of alternatives is not the same object as a bundle drawn from a continuum of goods.
 For bundle demand,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{basket-measures}{\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G})}$
\end_inset

 be the finite nonnegative Radon measures on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}$
\end_inset

.
 A basket is 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

,
 a price schedule is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in C_{++}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

,
 and expenditure is the dual pairing
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{continuum-pairing}{\langle p,x\rangle}
:=\int_{\mathcal{G}}p(g)\,x(dg).
\]

\end_inset

The budget is 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\in\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G}):\langle p,x\rangle\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
 An observed finite basket is included exactly as the atomic measure 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\sum_{j=1}^{J}q_{j}\delta_{g_{j}}$
\end_inset

,
 for which expenditure is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j}p(g_{j})q_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 A density 
\begin_inset Formula $q(g)$
\end_inset

 is the special case 
\begin_inset Formula $x(dg)=q(g)\nu(dg)$
\end_inset

 relative to a reference measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

.
 The measure formulation therefore contains both finite baskets and genuinely diffuse consumption
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "mascolell1975,bewley1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neiman and Vavra use a continuum of products for tractability,
 but their consumer chooses quantities of many varieties in an endogenous subset and incurs a utility cost of expanding that subset.
 Their heterogeneity is a mixture of common and idiosyncratic product ranks.
 It is not a metric product geometry:
 adjacent ranks need not be nearby in characteristics.
 Their framework therefore supplies selection and variety mechanisms,
 not a theorem that a price change diverts demand to nearby products
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "neimanvavra2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Neighborhood compensated elasticity.
 The radius-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 neighborhood of product point 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{product-neighborhood}{B_{r}(g)}:=\{g'\in\mathcal{G}:d(g,g')\leq r\}.
\]

\end_inset

Let locally unique Hicksian demand be a measure,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $h(A;p,\overline u)>0$
\end_inset

 denote its mass in measurable region 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Fix a family of continuous log-price shock profiles 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{B}:\mathcal{G}\to[0,1]$
\end_inset

 indexed by closed regions 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 normalized by 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{B}=1$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 and by a common support rule,
 for example 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{supp}\varphi_{B}\subseteq\{g:d(g,B)\leq\rho\}$
\end_inset

 for a fixed bandwidth 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

.
 Set 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\varepsilon}(g)=p(g)\exp\{\varepsilon\varphi_{B}(g)\}$
\end_inset

.
 If the scalar path 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon\mapsto h(A;p_{\varepsilon},\overline u)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable at zero,
 define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{neighborhood-elasticity}{\mathcal{E}^{H}(A;\varphi_{B})}
:=\left.\frac{d\log h(A;p_{\varepsilon},\overline u)}{d\varepsilon}\right|_{\varepsilon=0}.
\]

\end_inset

In a finite catalog with the discrete topology,
 one may take 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{B}(g)=\mathbb{I}\{g\in B\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This statistic replaces an unmanageable product-by-product elasticity matrix with a region-to-region response.
 It also avoids an inadmissible discontinuous point shock when prices must remain continuous;
 for diffuse baskets,
 a singleton additionally has zero mass.
 For fixed-radius separated closed regions and profiles with the same amplitude and bandwidth,
 a precise locality restriction uses a nonincreasing envelope 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

 satisfying
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\mathcal{E}^{H}(A;\varphi_{B})\right|
\leq\Psi\!\left(d(A,\operatorname{supp}\varphi_{B})\right).
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $d(A,C):=\inf\{d(a,c):a\in A,\ c\in C\}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

The absolute value permits either substitution or complementarity;
 the sign records which one obtains.
 Such decay is an additional economic restriction.
 It does not follow from continuity,
 utility maximization,
 or the existence of a continuum.
 With bundles,
 some neighborhoods may instead be complements;
 compensated and Marshallian cross effects must also be kept distinct.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Finite-data Afriat logic is dimension-free
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The finite-data logic of Afriat's theorem survives this change of commodity space.
 What the data use is not a coordinate count but the finite matrix of observed expenditures.
 The functional-analytic assumptions below supply the global utility and demand-existence claims that are automatic in a bounded finite-dimensional presentation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Afriat rationalization over a continuum product space.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{G},d)$
\end_inset

 be compact metric,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

 be the cone of finite nonnegative Radon measures in 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\mathcal{G})^{*}$
\end_inset

 equipped with the weak-star topology 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(C(\mathcal{G})^{*},C(\mathcal{G}))$
\end_inset

,
 let bundles lie in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

,
 and let each observed price schedule 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\in C_{++}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the finite dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{(p^{t},x^{t})\}_{t=1}^{T}$
\end_inset

 has through-observation budget
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B_{t}:=\{x\in\mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathcal{G}):
\langle p^{t},x\rangle\leq\langle p^{t},x^{t}\rangle\}.
\]

\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\mathrel{R^{D}}x^{s}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{s}\in B_{t}$
\end_inset

,
 define 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\mathrel{P^{D}}x^{s}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle p^{t},x^{s}\rangle<\langle p^{t},x^{t}\rangle$
\end_inset

,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be the transitive closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}$
\end_inset

.
 Say that the dataset satisfies GARP if,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\mathrel{R}x^{s}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(x^{s}\mathrel{P^{D}}x^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 GARP holds if and only if there exist numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $(U^{t},\lambda^{t})_{t=1}^{T}$
\end_inset

 satisfying the Afriat inequalities
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U^{s}\leq U^{t}+\lambda^{t}\langle p^{t},x^{s}-x^{t}\rangle,
\qquad \lambda^{t}>0,
\qquad s,t=1,\ldots,T.
\]

\end_inset

For any such certificate,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(x):=\min_{1\leq t\leq T}
\left\{U^{t}+\lambda^{t}\langle p^{t},x-x^{t}\rangle\right\}
\]

\end_inset

is concave,
 weak-star continuous,
 and strictly increasing in the measure order,
 and it rationalizes every observation.
 Moreover,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in C_{++}(\mathcal{G})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 attains a maximum on 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\geq0:\langle p,x\rangle\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Apply the finite-expenditure-matrix form of Afriat's lemma to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{ts}:=\langle p^{t},x^{s}\rangle$
\end_inset

.
 This algebraic lemma says that GARP for the finite comparison graph is equivalent to the displayed certificate;
 it does not use the dimension of the ambient commodity space.
 At observation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{s}$
\end_inset

,
 every affine term in the displayed minimum is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $U^{s}$
\end_inset

 and term 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 equals 
\begin_inset Formula $U^{s}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x^{s})=U^{s}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle p^{s},x\rangle\leq\langle p^{s},x^{s}\rangle$
\end_inset

,
 term 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x)\leq U^{s}=U(x^{s})$
\end_inset

,
 which proves rationalization.
 A finite minimum of continuous affine functionals is concave and weak-star continuous.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y-x$
\end_inset

 is a nonzero positive measure,
 compactness and strict positivity give 
\begin_inset Formula $\min_{g\in\mathcal{G}}p^{t}(g)>0$
\end_inset

 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{t}\langle p^{t},y-x\rangle>0$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is finite,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta:=\min_{1\leq t\leq T}
\lambda^{t}\langle p^{t},y-x\rangle>0.
\]

\end_inset

Consequently 
\begin_inset Formula $U(y)\geq U(x)+\delta>U(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\min_{g}p(g)>0$
\end_inset

,
 every budget-feasible measure satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\mathcal{G})\leq w/m$
\end_inset

.
 The positive budget set is weak-star closed and is contained in the closed dual norm ball of radius 
\begin_inset Formula $w/m$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $C(\mathcal{G})^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 That ball is weak-star compact by Banach--Alaoglu,
 so the budget set is weak-star compact as a closed subset.
 Continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 gives attainment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
The proposition is a dimension-free corollary of the ordinary Afriat construction,
 not a claim that all infinite-dimensional consumer theory is automatic.
 It uses divisible measure-valued bundles,
 continuous strictly positive linear prices,
 compact product space,
 and finitely many observations.
 Indivisible unit demand,
 arbitrary nonlinear budgets,
 equilibrium existence,
 or differentiable Slutsky operators require separate arguments
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "afriat1967,varian1982,mascolell1975,bewley1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
When product choice becomes matching
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With unlimited replication at posted prices,
 consumers independently choosing products is a choice model with assignment geometry;
 it is not yet a matching market.
 Matching becomes literal when products,
 sellers,
 or qualities have capacity.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

 be probability measures on compact spaces 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 of consumer types and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}$
\end_inset

 of products,
 respectively,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $S(b,g)$
\end_inset

 be continuous transferable surplus,
 and define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi(\mu,\nu):=\{\pi:\pi_{1}=\mu,\ \pi_{2}=\nu\}.
\]

\end_inset

The planner's assignment problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\int_{\mathcal{B}\times\mathcal{G}}S(b,g)\,d\pi(b,g).
\]

\end_inset

Compactness and continuity give existence.
 The fixed second marginal exhausts supply;
 a model with upper capacities or unmatched agents instead needs marginal inequalities,
 partial transport,
 or dummy outside types.
 This is the planner problem underlying transferable-utility hedonic matching.
 A market interpretation additionally requires participation conditions and Kantorovich dual potentials that support the assignment as utilities and prices.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 is absolutely continuous,
 additional twist and regularity conditions can yield an almost-everywhere pure match;
 without them the optimal plan need not be a function.
 This capacity-constrained problem is conceptually distinct from the one-to-one deferred-acceptance model studied later in the book
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chiapporimccannnesheim2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evidence and the modern research map
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neiman and Vavra's Nielsen Homescan evidence covers 2004--2016.
 Within Nielsen product groups,
 each household's annual UPC spending becomes more concentrated while aggregate UPC spending becomes less concentrated:
 households buy narrower but increasingly different baskets.
 The model infers roughly 70 percent growth in effective variety.
 In a counterfactual that holds taste disagreement fixed and attributes that growth entirely to catalog variety,
 welfare rises by 0.56 percent per year in the covered categories;
 0.52 percentage points,
 or about 93 percent of that gain,
 comes from improved selection rather than conventional love of variety.
 That variety-only exercise does not reproduce the observed decline in the number of varieties per household.
 Specifications that target all three trends imply annual welfare gains of 0.45--0.47 percent.
 These are structural model implications rather than direct causal estimates.
 In particular,
 household concentration,
 aggregate concentration,
 and the observed average number of varieties consumed jointly identify the dispersion parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 and effective variety 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde N=N\alpha/(1-\alpha)$
\end_inset

,
 not catalog size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 separately from taste disagreement 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "neimanvavra2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature divides naturally by the economic object:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Characteristics and ideal points explain how a dense catalog acquires geometry;
 pure-characteristics and local-competition models turn that geometry into substitution restrictions
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
