key "lancaster1966,berrypakes2007,feenstralevinsohn1995,pinksesladebrett2002"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Random-coefficients demand estimates heterogeneous tastes and flexible diversion among a large but finite set of products
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "berrylevinsohnpakes1995"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Variety and long-tail models distinguish ordinary love of variety,
 access costs,
 and better matching to household tastes
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "brynjolfssonhusmith2003,brynjolfssonhusimester2011,brodaweinstein2010,li2021,neimanvavra2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consideration-set methods distinguish products that are available from products that enter the consumer's consideration set
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "abaluckadamsprassl2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Zero-share methods address available products that receive zero observed empirical market share;
 they do not by themselves identify whether a consumer saw the product
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gandhilushi2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hedonic matching and optimal transport add capacity,
 fixed supply marginals,
 and market-clearing assignments to the product-space geometry;
 endogenous quality design additionally requires producer technology and costs
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chiapporimccannnesheim2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model-selection rule is therefore simple.
 If the consumer buys one item,
 use discrete choice on product space.
 If the consumer buys a basket,
 use measure-valued demand.
 If product capacities constrain who can receive what,
 use matching or optimal transport.
 In every case,
 product nearness must be given economic content before it can discipline cross-price effects.
\end_layout


