\begin_layout Section
From accounts to counterfactuals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Computable general equilibrium (CGE) analysis uses a calibrated microeconomic model and a social accounting matrix to evaluate policy counterfactuals.
 Calibration chooses parameters so that the model reproduces a benchmark allocation and the associated accounting identities before the policy is changed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A two-sector small open economy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let sectors be indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathcal{J}:=\{1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

 and households by 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in\mathcal{H}:=\{1,\ldots,H\}$
\end_inset

;
 the benchmark application has 
\begin_inset Formula $J=2$
\end_inset

 goods.
 Sector 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 uses labor 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{j}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and sector-specific capital 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{j}\geq0$
\end_inset

 to produce output 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 The corresponding aggregate factor endowments are 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{K}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}>0$
\end_inset

 denote the domestic price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{P}:=(P_{1},\ldots,P_{J})$
\end_inset

 the domestic price vector,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 the wage,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{j}$
\end_inset

 the rental rate on capital specific to sector 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 A small open economy takes world prices as given.
 For sector 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 a constant-elasticity-of-substitution production function is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{j}=A_{j}\left[\alpha_{j}K^{-\rho_{j}}_{j}+(1-\alpha_{j})n^{-\rho_{j}}_{j}\right]^{-1/\rho_{j}},
\]

\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{j}>0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\alpha_{j}<1$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{j}>-1,\ \rho_{j}\ne0$
\end_inset

.
 The associated elasticity parameter is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{j}=1/(1+\rho_{j})>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
(Cost and conditional factor demand.) Given output 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{Q}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and factor prices 
\begin_inset Formula $(r_{j},W)$
\end_inset

,
 the unit-consistent cost function is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{j}(r_{j},W,\bar{Q}_{j})=\min_{K_{j},n_{j}\geq0}\{r_{j}K_{j}+Wn_{j}:Q_{j}(K_{j},n_{j})\geq\bar{Q}_{j}\}.
\]

\end_inset

 When differentiable,
 conditional factor demands satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K^{D}_{j}=\frac{\partial c_{j}}{\partial r_{j}},\qquad n^{D}_{j}=\frac{\partial c_{j}}{\partial W}.
\]

\end_inset

 This derivative formulation avoids confusing the price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}$
\end_inset

 with the CES parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Social accounting and calibration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The social accounting matrix records production,
 factor payments,
 household income,
 and expenditure flows that must balance at the benchmark.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{h}$
\end_inset

 denote household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

's nominal income and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{hj}\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 be its expenditure share on good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\beta_{hj}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Under Cobb--Douglas preferences,
 household demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{hj}(\boldsymbol{P},Y_{h})$
\end_inset

 satisfies the expenditure equation 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}x_{hj}(\boldsymbol{P},Y_{h})=\beta_{hj}Y_{h}$
\end_inset

,
 so the observed benchmark share identifies 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{hj}=P_{j}x_{hj}(\boldsymbol{P},Y_{h})/Y_{h}$
\end_inset

.
 The calibrated model is checked by substituting the parameter values back into every accounting identity and market-clearing condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Equilibrium closure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A computable model is not defined by technology and accounting identities alone.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{n}_{h}$
\end_inset

 be household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

's labor endowment and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{K}_{hj}$
\end_inset

 its endowment of capital specific to sector 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\bar{n}_{h}=\bar{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\bar{K}_{hj}=\bar{K}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Household income must specify factor ownership,
 taxes,
 transfers,
 and the disposition of profits and tariff revenue.
 Writing 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset

 for transfers,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{h}$
\end_inset

 for distributed profits,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{Tax}_{h}$
\end_inset

 for direct taxes,
 a representative identity is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{h}=W\bar{n}_{h}+\sum_{j\in\mathcal{J}}r_{j}\bar{K}_{hj}+T_{h}+\Pi_{h}-\operatorname{Tax}_{h}.
\]

\end_inset

 Demand equations 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{hj}=x_{hj}(\boldsymbol{P},Y_{h})$
\end_inset

 then close the household side.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each good 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathcal{J}$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{j}$
\end_inset

 denote imports and exports,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\operatorname{Inv}_{j}$
\end_inset

 investment demand,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{j}$
\end_inset

 government final demand.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{jk}\geq0$
\end_inset

 denote the amount of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 used as an intermediate input per unit of sector 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's output.
 The resource balance is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{j}+M_{j}=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}x_{hj}(\boldsymbol{P},Y_{h})+G_{j}+\operatorname{Inv}_{j}+E_{j}+\sum_{k\in\mathcal{J}}a_{jk}Q_{k}.
\]

\end_inset

 Factor markets satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{j\in\mathcal{J}}n_{j}=\bar{n}=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\bar{n}_{h},\qquad K_{j}=\bar{K}_{j}=\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\bar{K}_{hj}\quad\text{for sector-specific capital}.
\]

\end_inset

 Constant returns and competitive pricing impose zero profit,
 so the domestic producer price equals unit cost whenever the sector operates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government budget identifies the use of tax and tariff receipts,
 and the external account identifies the financing of net imports.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{W}_{j}>0$
\end_inset

 denote the world price of traded good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{ER}>0$
\end_inset

 denote the exchange rate measured in units of domestic currency per unit of foreign currency.
 A balanced-trade closure,
 for example,
 imposes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{j\in\mathcal{J}}P^{W}_{j}M_{j}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{J}}P^{W}_{j}E_{j},
\]

\end_inset

 whereas a fixed foreign transfer replaces the right-hand side by exports plus that transfer.
 The model must choose one nominal normalization,
 such as 
\begin_inset Formula $W=1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{ER}=1$
\end_inset

,
 and must not impose both as independent equilibrium equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A benchmark replication check solves this entire system at the calibrated parameters and reports the maximum absolute residual in every household,
 firm,
 market,
 government,
 and external-account equation.
 Counterfactual welfare must also be declared,
 for example equivalent variation relative to the benchmark utility and price system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Counterfactual policies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an imported good 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathcal{J}$
\end_inset

 that is a perfect substitute for the domestic good,
 the law of one price with an ad valorem tariff 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{j}\geq0$
\end_inset

 changes the domestic price according to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{j}=\mathrm{ER}\,P^{W}_{j}(1+\tau_{j}).
\]

\end_inset

 Free trade is the baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{j}=0$
\end_inset

;
 a tariff counterfactual changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{j}$
\end_inset

 and resolves the full equilibrium system.
 Other counterfactuals change tax,
 subsidy,
 transfer,
 or technology equations while leaving the accounting identities and calibrated preference and technology parameters in place.
\end_layout


