\begin_layout Standard
This mathematical appendix collects the order-theoretic concepts used throughout the book.
 The orientation is economic:
 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is ranked at least as high as 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Unless stated otherwise,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a nonempty set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Binary Relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $R\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

.
 We write 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)\in R$
\end_inset

.
 The inverse relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{inverse-relation}{R^{-1}}$
\end_inset

 is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $xR^{-1}y\iff yRx$
\end_inset

.
 The identity relation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{identity-relation}{\Delta_{X}}=\{(x,x):x\in X\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A relation 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Reflexive if 
\begin_inset Formula $xRx$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Complete (or total) if,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in X$
\end_inset

,
 either 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $yRx$
\end_inset

 (or both).
 Completeness implies reflexivity by taking 
\begin_inset Formula $x=y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Symmetric if 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $yRx$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Asymmetric if 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(yRx)$
\end_inset

.
 Every asymmetric relation is irreflexive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Antisymmetric if 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $yRx$
\end_inset

 imply 
\begin_inset Formula $x=y$
\end_inset

.
 Antisymmetry does not mean the negation of symmetry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Transitive if 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $yRz$
\end_inset

 imply 
\begin_inset Formula $xRz$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Acyclic if there do not exist 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0},\ldots,x_{n}\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}=x_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k-1}Rx_{k}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
 Acyclicity is normally imposed on a strict relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The equality relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is reflexive,
 symmetric,
 transitive,
 and antisymmetric,
 but it is incomplete whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 contains two distinct elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Important Classes of Relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
An equivalence relation is reflexive,
 symmetric,
 and transitive.
 A preorder is reflexive and transitive.
 A partial order is reflexive,
 transitive,
 and antisymmetric.
 A total preorder is a complete preorder.
 A linear order (or total order) is a complete partial order.
 A set equipped with a partial order is a partially ordered set,
 or poset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In consumer theory,
 a rational weak preference relation is modeled as a total preorder.
 Antisymmetry is generally inappropriate for weak preference because distinct alternatives may be indifferent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The usual relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

 is a linear order on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 The componentwise relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy\iff x_{\ell}\geq y_{\ell}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

,
 is a partial order but is not complete when 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq2$
\end_inset

.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,2)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(2,1)$
\end_inset

 are incomparable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The lexicographic relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $xR_{\mathrm{lex}}y$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}>y_{1}$
\end_inset

 or if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}\geq y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 It is a linear order.
 Although complete and transitive,
 it has no continuous real-valued representation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 with its usual topology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Symmetric and Asymmetric Parts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The symmetric part 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 and asymmetric part 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{strict-part}{P_{R}}$
\end_inset

 of a relation 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 are defined by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
xI_{R}y\iff xRy\text{ and }yRx,\qquad xP_{R}y\iff xRy\text{ and }\neg(yRx).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is a preorder,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{R}$
\end_inset

 is asymmetric and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Reflexivity and symmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 follow immediately,
 and its transitivity follows from transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Asymmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{R}$
\end_inset

 is built into its definition.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $xP_{R}y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $yP_{R}z$
\end_inset

,
 then transitivity gives 
\begin_inset Formula $xRz$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $zRx$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $zRy$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting 
\begin_inset Formula $yP_{R}z$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $xP_{R}z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Equivalence Classes and Quotient Orders
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 be an equivalence relation.
 The equivalence class of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{equivalence-class}{[x]_{E}}=\{y\in X:yEx\}$
\end_inset

.
 The quotient set is 
\begin_inset Formula $X/E=\{[x]_{E}:x\in X\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
If 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{a,b,c\}$
\end_inset

 and the only distinct equivalent elements are 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $X/E=\{\{a,b\},\{c\}\}$
\end_inset

.
 For a preference preorder,
 the equivalence classes of 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 are indifference classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be a preorder and let 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 be its symmetric part.
 On 
\begin_inset Formula $X/I_{R}$
\end_inset

,
 define 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[x]_{I_{R}}\,\notationfirst{quotient-order}{\bar{R}}\,[y]_{I_{R}}\iff xRy.
\]

\end_inset

Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset

 is well defined and is a partial order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $xI_{R}x'$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $yI_{R}y'$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x'Rx$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $yRy'$
\end_inset

;
 transitivity therefore gives 
\begin_inset Formula $x'Ry'$
\end_inset

,
 which proves that the definition is independent of representatives.
 Reflexivity and transitivity are inherited from 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 If both 
\begin_inset Formula $[x]\bar{R}[y]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $[y]\bar{R}[x]$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $xI_{R}y$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $[x]=[y]$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset

 is antisymmetric.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Maximal and Greatest Elements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
For a nonempty subset 
\begin_inset Formula $S\subseteq X$
\end_inset

,
 the set of
\series bold
maximal elements
\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{maximal-elements}{\operatorname{Max}(S,R)}=\{x\in S:\nexists y\in S\text{ such that }yP_{R}x\}.
\]

\end_inset

The set of
\series bold
greatest elements
\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{greatest-elements}{\operatorname{Great}(S,R)}=\{x\in S:xRy\text{ for every }y\in S\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A greatest element dominates every feasible alternative;
 a maximal element is merely undominated.
 Every greatest element is maximal.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is complete on 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 then the two notions coincide.
 Under a partial order there is at most one greatest element,
 while there may be many maximal elements.
 Under a preorder,
 several indifferent alternatives may all be greatest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S=\{a,b,c\}$
\end_inset

 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\Delta_{S}\cup\{(a,b)\}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $aP_{R}b$
\end_inset

 and every other pair of distinct alternatives is incomparable.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 are maximal,
 but there is no greatest element because 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 are incomparable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Every nonempty finite subset of a set equipped with a total preorder has at least one greatest element and therefore at least one maximal element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Proceed by induction on the cardinality of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 The claim is immediate for a singleton.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is greatest in a finite set 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 and add 
\begin_inset Formula $a\notin S$
\end_inset

.
 Completeness gives either 
\begin_inset Formula $aRg$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $gRa$
\end_inset

.
 In the first case,
 transitivity and 
\begin_inset Formula $gRy$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in S$
\end_inset

 imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 is greatest in 
\begin_inset Formula $S\cup\{a\}$
\end_inset

.
 In the second case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 remains greatest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finiteness matters.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{R},\geq)$
\end_inset

 is linearly ordered but has no greatest element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Transitive Closure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
For relations 
\begin_inset Formula $Q,R\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

,
 composition is oriented by 
\begin_inset Formula $x(R\circ Q)z\iff\exists y\in X:\ xQy\text{ and }yRz$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{relation-powers}{R^{1}}=R$
\end_inset

,
 and recursively define 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{n+1}=R^{n}\circ R$
\end_inset

.
 The transitive closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\notationfirst{transitive-closure}{R^{+}}=\bigcup_{n\geq1}R^{n}.
\]

\end_inset

The reflexive-transitive closure is 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{reflexive-transitive-closure}{R^{*}}=\Delta_{X}\cup R^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{X}$
\end_inset

 is the identity relation defined above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The relation 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{+}$
\end_inset

 is the smallest transitive relation containing 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Concatenating an 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

-path of length 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 with one of length 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 yields a path of length 
\begin_inset Formula $m+n$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{+}$
\end_inset

 is transitive.
 Any transitive relation containing 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 must contain every 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{n}$
\end_inset

 and hence their union.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Order Extensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be a preorder on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 We call 
\begin_inset Formula $\notationfirst{order-extension}{R^{\mathrm{ext}}}$
\end_inset

 an extension of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $R\subseteq R^{\mathrm{ext}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{R}\subseteq P_{R^{\mathrm{ext}}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus every original weak and strict comparison is preserved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(Szpilrajn extension theorem) Every partial order on a set has a linear-order extension.
 See
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "szpilrajn1930"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Proof sketch.
 The standard proof applies Zorn's lemma to the set of partial orders extending the given order,
 ordered by inclusion;
 this use of Zorn invokes the axiom of choice.
 The union of every chain is again a partial order,
 so a maximal extension exists.
 The key extension lemma is that if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 are incomparable,
 adjoining 
\begin_inset Formula $xRy$
\end_inset

 and taking the transitive closure preserves antisymmetry:
 a new nontrivial cycle would contain the added edge and would imply an original path from 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 back to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting incomparability.
 Thus two incomparable elements could be consistently compared,
 contradicting maximality.
 Hence the maximal extension is complete and therefore linear.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
Every preorder has a total-preorder extension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{R}$
\end_inset

 be the symmetric part of the preorder 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 The induced relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X/I_{R}$
\end_inset

 is a partial order by the earlier proposition.
 Next,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}^{\mathrm{ext}}$
\end_inset

 be a linear-order extension of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset

,
 and define on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
xR^{\mathrm{ext}}y\iff[x]_{I_{R}}\,\bar{R}^{\mathrm{ext}}\,[y]_{I_{R}}.
\]

\end_inset

Then 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\mathrm{ext}}$
\end_inset

 is a total preorder.
 It contains 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 and antisymmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}^{\mathrm{ext}}$
\end_inset

 ensures that every comparison in 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{R}$
\end_inset

 remains strict.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Checklist for Economic Applications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use a total preorder for rational weak preference;
 use its asymmetric part for strict preference and its symmetric part for indifference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Do not call a strict relation a partial order:
 strict relations are irreflexive,
 whereas partial orders are reflexive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Distinguish maximal from greatest alternatives whenever the relation may be incomplete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use quotient notation 
\begin_inset Formula $X/I_{R}$
\end_inset

,
 not set-difference notation,
 when collapsing indifference classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
State finiteness,
 compactness,
 continuity,
 or another attainment condition whenever existence of a maximal or greatest alternative is claimed.
\end_layout

