\begin_layout Section
WGARP and Coherent CMU
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Result ID:
 CT-RP-WGARP-CMU.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq2$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{(p^{t},x^{t})\}_{t\in\mathbb{T}}$
\end_inset

 be finite,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}_{++}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\in X\setminus\{0\}$
\end_inset

,
 and observed wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{t}=p^{t}\cdot x^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Equip 
\begin_inset Formula $C(X)$
\end_inset

 with the topology of uniform convergence on compact subsets.
 A compact coalition structure is a compact collection 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 of nonempty compact subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $C(X)$
\end_inset

,
 under the induced Hausdorff topology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Full WGARP--CMU characterization.
 Under the preceding displayed environment,
 the following six statements are equivalent.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thmfull:wgarp-cmu"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The data are rationalized by an asymmetric,
 strictly increasing,
 continuous preference function 
\begin_inset Formula $r:X\times X\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The data are rationalized by a coherent,
 strictly increasing,
 continuous CMU function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The data are rationalized by a skew-symmetric,
 coherent,
 continuous CMU function whose coalitions consist of strictly increasing concave utilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The data satisfy WGARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For every 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t\in\mathbb{T}$
\end_inset

 there exist 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{s,t}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{s}_{s,t},\lambda^{t}_{s,t}>0$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R^{s,t}=-R^{t,s},\qquad\lambda^{s}_{s,t}=\lambda^{s}_{t,s},\qquad R^{s,t}\geq\lambda^{s}_{s,t}p^{s}\cdot(x^{s}-x^{t}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There are antisymmetric numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{s,t}=-V^{t,s}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned}p^{s}\cdot(x^{s}-x^{t})\geq0 & \Longrightarrow V^{s,t}\geq0,\\
p^{s}\cdot(x^{s}-x^{t})>0 & \Longrightarrow V^{s,t}>0
\end{aligned}
\qquad(s,t\in\mathbb{T}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof record.
 The easy implications from a rationalizing preference function to WGARP and between the two numerical certificate systems are finite arguments.
 The reverse representation direction constructs symmetric pairwise Afriat utilities and assembles them into compact coherent coalitions.
 That research-level construction,
 including compactness and skew-symmetry,
 is proof-delegated to the exact finite-data theorem of
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "aguiarhjertstrandserranoevren2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 this book does not relabel the delegation as an independent proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Boundary note.
 Coherence means pairwise intersection and need not imply a common utility in every coalition.
 A general coherent CMU may be incomplete or intransitive.
 The representation rationalizes the observed budgets but does not guarantee nonempty demand at all counterfactual budgets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
WARP and Strict Coherent CMU
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Result ID:
 CT-RP-WARP-CMU.
 A preference function is strictly star-concave when,
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r(\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y,x)>\alpha r(x,x)+(1-\alpha)r(y,x).
\]

\end_inset

This condition concerns the first argument along line segments ending at the second argument;
 it is not global concavity of the two-argument function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Full WARP--CMU characterization.
 In the same finite budget environment,
 the following are equivalent:
 (i) WARP;
 (ii) rationalization by an asymmetric,
 strictly star-concave,
 continuous preference function;
 (iii) rationalization by a coherent,
 strictly star-concave,
 continuous CMU;
 and (iv) rationalization by a skew-symmetric,
 coherent,
 continuous CMU whose coalitions consist of strictly increasing strictly concave utilities.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thmfull:warp-cmu"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof record.
 The proof uses strict pairwise utility rationalizations and is separately delegated to the WARP theorem and online appendix of
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "aguiarhjertstrandserranoevren2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It is not obtained by merely replacing the acronym WGARP in the preceding theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Formalization Record and Boundaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
CT-RP-CMU-SINGLETON.
 Textbook proof complete;
 local Lean target.
 The exact singleton reduction is elementary and introduces no compactness machinery beyond the singleton case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
CT-RP-CMU-CLD.
 Lean core verified through the pinned WGARP project and the compiled declaration 
\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{Econ803.Consumer.compensated\_law\_of\_demand}$
\end_inset

.
 The certificate covers the asymmetric,
 strictly increasing preference-function result stated in the chapter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
CT-RP-WGARP-CMU and CT-RP-WARP-CMU.
 Cited theorems,
 not yet Lean complete.
 A complete formalization requires the pairwise Afriat construction,
 compact-open and Hausdorff topology,
 coherence,
 and the strict-rationalization dependencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model does not by itself recover transitivity,
 global convexity,
 classical UMP--EMP duality,
 Slutsky symmetry,
 scalar-utility welfare comparisons,
 or global existence of counterfactual demand.
 Those conclusions remain owned by the benchmark chapters and require their displayed assumptions.
\end_layout

